r"""Rich-text versions of the catalog's prose fields (used by tools/build_catalog.py).

The catalog phase wrote its prose with ASCII math ("mean lambda", "sigma^2", "(1-p)^k").
These replacements say the same thing in the site's rich-text format: HTML in which $...$ is
inline TeX. build_catalog.py substitutes them into the generated catalog and also derives plain
unicode versions (fields ending in `Text`) for places that cannot show TeX (aria labels, titles).

Keys: distribution id; "<distribution id>.<param key>"; relation id; notation sym.
"""

PARAMETERIZATION = {
 'discrete-uniform': r"Integers $a \le b$. Each of the $n = b - a + 1$ values $a, a+1, \dots, b$ has probability $1/n$. Some texts use the support $\{1, \dots, n\}$ (that is, $a = 1$, $b = n$) or $\{0, \dots, n-1\}$.",
 'bernoulli': r"Success probability $p$: $P(X = 1) = p$ and $P(X = 0) = 1 - p$.",
 'categorical': r"$K$ categories labelled $1, \dots, K$ with probability vector $\mathbf p = (p_1, \dots, p_K)$, where every $p_j \ge 0$ and $\sum_j p_j = 1$. On this page the probabilities are set by dragging bars; they start equal ($p_j = 1/K$) and are rescaled so that they always add up to 1. Some texts code the outcome as a one-hot vector, which is the same as $\mathrm{Multinomial}(1, \mathbf p)$.",
 'binomial': r"Number of successes $X$ in $n$ independent trials, each with success probability $p$.",
 'multinomial': r"$n$ trials, $K$ categories and a probability vector $\mathbf p = (p_1, \dots, p_K)$ that adds up to 1 (set on this page by dragging bars; equal by default). The outcome is the vector of counts $\mathbf X = (X_1, \dots, X_K)$, where $X_j$ is the number of trials that land in category $j$.",
 'hypergeometric': r"A population of $N$ items, of which $K$ count as successes. $n$ items are drawn without replacement, and $X$ is the number of successes drawn. Software uses different letters: R uses $m = K$, $n = N - K$ and $k$ = number of draws; SciPy uses $M = N$, $n = K$ and $N$ = number of draws.",
 'geometric': r"$K$ = the number of failures before the first success in independent $\mathrm{Bernoulli}(p)$ trials: $P(K = k) = (1-p)^k p$ for $k = 0, 1, 2, \dots$, with mean $(1-p)/p$. The other common convention counts trials instead: $Y = K + 1$ is the number of trials up to and including the first success, with support $\{1, 2, \dots\}$ and mean $1/p$. This site counts failures so that $\mathrm{Geometric}(p) = \mathrm{NegBin}(1, p)$.",
 'negative-binomial': r"$K$ = the number of failures before the $r$-th success in independent $\mathrm{Bernoulli}(p)$ trials: $P(K = k) = \binom{k+r-1}{k} p^r (1-p)^k$, with mean $r(1-p)/p$ and variance $r(1-p)/p^2$. $r$ may be any positive real number (this comes from the gamma–Poisson mixture). Other forms: the number of trials until the $r$-th success; and the mean–dispersion form used in regression software (R <code>glm.nb</code>, Stan <code>neg_binomial_2</code>) with mean $\mu = r(1-p)/p$ and variance $\mu + \mu^2/r$.",
 'poisson': r"Rate (mean) $\lambda$: $P(X = k) = e^{-\lambda} \lambda^k / k!$, and the mean and the variance both equal $\lambda$. For events that occur at rate $\lambda$ per unit time, the count in a window of length $t$ is $\mathrm{Poisson}(\lambda t)$.",
 'beta-binomial': r"$n$ trials; $p \sim \mathrm{Beta}(\alpha, \beta)$ and, given $p$, $X \sim \mathrm{Binomial}(n, p)$. Then $P(X = k) = \binom nk B(k + \alpha,\, n - k + \beta) / B(\alpha, \beta)$. An alternative form uses the mean $\pi = \alpha/(\alpha + \beta)$ and the overdispersion $\rho = 1/(\alpha + \beta + 1)$.",
 'uniform': r"Lower bound $a$ and upper bound $b$; the density is $1/(b - a)$ on $[a, b]$.",
 'exponential': r"Rate $\lambda$: $f(x) = \lambda e^{-\lambda x}$, with mean $1/\lambda$. Alternative: the scale (mean) $\theta = 1/\lambda$, used by SciPy and NumPy.",
 'gamma': r"Shape $\alpha$ (also written $k$) and rate $\beta$: $f(x) = \beta^\alpha x^{\alpha-1} e^{-\beta x} / \Gamma(\alpha)$, with mean $\alpha/\beta$ and variance $\alpha/\beta^2$. This is the convention of <i>Bayesian Data Analysis</i> and Stan. Alternative: shape $k$ and scale $\theta = 1/\beta$ (Wikipedia's $(k, \theta)$, SciPy, NumPy).",
 'weibull': r"Shape $k$ and scale $\lambda$: $F(x) = 1 - \exp\!\big(-(x/\lambda)^k\big)$. With $k < 1$ the hazard (failure rate) decreases over time; $k = 1$ is the exponential with rate $1/\lambda$; with $k > 1$ the hazard increases; $k = 2$ is the Rayleigh distribution with $\sigma = \lambda/\sqrt 2$. Note that $\lambda$ is a scale here, not a rate.",
 'normal': r"Mean $\mu$ and standard deviation $\sigma$, written $\mathcal N(\mu, \sigma^2)$. Alternatives: the variance $\sigma^2$, or the precision $\tau = 1/\sigma^2$ (used in BUGS and JAGS).",
 'log-normal': r"$\mu$ and $\sigma$ are the mean and standard deviation of $\ln X$, not of $X$. The median of $X$ is $e^{\mu}$ and its mean is $e^{\mu + \sigma^2/2}$.",
 'student-t': r"The standard $t$ distribution with $\nu$ degrees of freedom (centered at 0, scale 1). The location–scale version $\mu + \sigma T$ is used for robust regression and as a prior. The mean is 0 when $\nu > 1$ and the variance is $\nu/(\nu - 2)$ when $\nu > 2$.",
 'chi-squared': r"Degrees of freedom $k$: the distribution of the sum of $k$ squared independent standard normal variables, with mean $k$ and variance $2k$. It equals $\mathrm{Gamma}(k/2,\ \text{rate } 1/2)$.",
 'beta': r"Shapes $\alpha$ and $\beta$: $f(x) = x^{\alpha-1} (1-x)^{\beta-1} / B(\alpha, \beta)$, with mean $\alpha/(\alpha + \beta)$. Alternative: the mean $m = \alpha/(\alpha + \beta)$ and the concentration $\kappa = \alpha + \beta$.",
 'f': r"Numerator degrees of freedom $d_1$ and denominator degrees of freedom $d_2$. The mean is $d_2/(d_2 - 2)$ when $d_2 > 2$.",
 'cauchy': r"Location $x_0$ (the median and the mode) and scale $\gamma$ (the half-width of the peak at half its height, which is also half the distance between the quartiles).",
 'half-cauchy': r"Scale $\gamma$: the distribution of $|X|$ for $X \sim \mathrm{Cauchy}(0, \gamma)$, with density $f(x) = \dfrac{2}{\pi \gamma \,(1 + (x/\gamma)^2)}$ and median $\gamma$. Gelman (2006) writes it $\mathrm{HalfCauchy}(0, A)$.",
 'laplace': r"Location $\mu$ and scale $b$: $f(x) = \exp(-|x - \mu|/b) / (2b)$, with variance $2b^2$. Alternative: the rate $\lambda = 1/b$.",
 'logistic': r"Location $\mu$ and scale $s$: $F(x) = 1 / \big(1 + e^{-(x - \mu)/s}\big)$, with variance $s^2 \pi^2 / 3$.",
 'inverse-gamma': r"Shape $\alpha$ and scale $\beta$: $f(x) = \beta^\alpha x^{-\alpha-1} e^{-\beta/x} / \Gamma(\alpha)$, with mean $\beta/(\alpha - 1)$ when $\alpha > 1$. If $X \sim \mathrm{Gamma}(\alpha,\ \text{rate } \beta)$ then $1/X \sim \mathrm{InvGamma}(\alpha,\ \text{scale } \beta)$.",
 'dirichlet': r"Concentration vector $\boldsymbol\alpha = (\alpha_1, \dots, \alpha_K)$ with every $\alpha_j > 0$, and total concentration $\alpha_0 = \sum_j \alpha_j$. The mean is $\mathrm E[X_j] = \alpha_j / \alpha_0$. The interactive view uses $K = 3$, shown on a triangle. Alternative: a mean vector $\mathbf m$ and the total concentration $\alpha_0$.",
 'multivariate-normal': r"Mean vector $\boldsymbol\mu$ in $\mathbb R^d$ and covariance matrix $\Sigma$ (symmetric and positive definite). The interactive view is bivariate ($d = 2$) with $\Sigma = \begin{pmatrix} \sigma_1^2 & \rho\sigma_1\sigma_2 \\ \rho\sigma_1\sigma_2 & \sigma_2^2 \end{pmatrix}$. Alternatives: the precision matrix $\Sigma^{-1}$; a Cholesky factor $L$ with $\Sigma = LL^\top$; in Stan, <code>multi_normal_cholesky</code> with an LKJ prior on the correlation matrix.",
 'von-mises': r"Mean direction $\mu$ and concentration $\kappa$: $f(x) = e^{\kappa \cos(x - \mu)} / \big(2\pi I_0(\kappa)\big)$, where $I_0$ is the modified Bessel function of order 0. $\kappa = 0$ is the uniform distribution on the circle; for large $\kappa$ it is approximately $\mathcal N(\mu, 1/\kappa)$.",
 'ex-gaussian': r"$\mu$ and $\sigma$ are the mean and standard deviation of the normal component; $\tau$ is the mean (scale) of the exponential component, whose rate is $1/\tau$. The overall mean is $\mu + \tau$ and the variance is $\sigma^2 + \tau^2$. Alternatives: the rate $\lambda = 1/\tau$; SciPy's <code>exponnorm</code> uses $K = \tau/\sigma$.",
 'wald': r"Mean $\mu$ and shape $\lambda$: $f(x) = \sqrt{\dfrac{\lambda}{2\pi x^3}}\, \exp\!\Big(-\dfrac{\lambda (x - \mu)^2}{2\mu^2 x}\Big)$, with variance $\mu^3/\lambda$. Evidence-accumulation form: a drift rate $v$, a threshold $a$ and a noise standard deviation $\sigma$ give $\mu = a/v$ and $\lambda = a^2/\sigma^2$. The name “inverse Gaussian” does not mean that $1/X$ is normal: the normal describes where a drifting random walk is at a fixed time, and the inverse Gaussian describes when it first reaches a fixed level. SciPy's <code>invgauss</code> uses the shape $\mu/\lambda$ with scale $\lambda$.",
 'pareto': r"Type I Pareto with scale (minimum) $x_m$ and tail index $\alpha$: $P(X > x) = (x_m/x)^\alpha$ for $x \ge x_m$, with mean $\alpha x_m / (\alpha - 1)$ when $\alpha > 1$. Alternative: the Lomax (Pareto type II) distribution, shifted to start at 0.",
 'gumbel': r"Maximum-type Gumbel with location $\mu$ and scale $\beta$: $F(x) = \exp\!\big(-e^{-(x - \mu)/\beta}\big)$, with mean $\mu + 0.5772\,\beta$. The minimum-type Gumbel is its mirror image.",
}

