RELATION_PLAIN = {
 'discrete-uniform--bernoulli': r"A fair coin flip is both a Bernoulli trial with $p = 1/2$ and a discrete uniform variable on the two values 0 and 1. The two families overlap only at this point: a Bernoulli with $p \ne 1/2$ is not uniform, and a discrete uniform on more than two values is not Bernoulli.",
 'bernoulli--binomial': r"Repeat the same yes/no trial $n$ times independently and count the successes; that count is binomial. A binomial with a single trial ($n = 1$) is just a Bernoulli trial.",
 'bernoulli--geometric': r"Keep running independent Bernoulli trials and count how many failures happen before the first success. That count is geometric: it equals $k$ only if the first $k$ trials fail (probability $(1-p)^k$) and the next one succeeds (probability $p$).",
 'hypergeometric--binomial': r"Drawing without replacement from a very large population is almost the same as drawing with replacement, because removing a few items barely changes the proportion of successes; so the hypergeometric approaches the binomial as the population size $N$ grows. In the other direction, if two groups each produce binomial counts with the same $p$, then given the total number of successes, the count in the first group is hypergeometric and no longer depends on $p$. This is the basis of Fisher's exact test.",
 'binomial--geometric': r"The original arrow is best read as two views of the same sequence of trials. The binomial fixes the number of trials and counts successes; the geometric fixes the number of successes (one) and counts how long you wait. Waiting at least $k$ failures for the first success is the same event as seeing zero successes in the first $k$ trials. The same duality links the negative binomial and the binomial: $P(\mathrm{NegBin}(r, p) \le k) = P(\mathrm{Bin}(k + r, p) \ge r)$.",
 'geometric--negative-binomial': r"Waiting for $r$ successes is the same as waiting for the first success $r$ times in a row. Adding $r$ independent geometric counts of failures gives the total number of failures before the $r$-th success, which is negative binomial. With $r = 1$ the negative binomial is the geometric.",
 'binomial--poisson': r"When there are many trials and each success is rare, with the expected number of successes $np$ held near $\lambda$, the binomial count is well approximated by a Poisson count with mean $\lambda$. This is the “law of rare events”, and it is why counts of rare independent events (mutations, spikes in short time bins, accidents) are often modeled as Poisson.",
 'poisson--exponential': r"In a Poisson process, events happen independently of each other at a constant average rate $\lambda$. The number of events in a window of length $t$ is Poisson with mean $\lambda t$, and the waiting times between successive events are independent exponentials with rate $\lambda$. Each description implies the other.",
 'geometric--exponential': r"This is the discrete-to-continuous limit of waiting times. Split each unit of time into $n$ tiny steps, each with a small chance $\lambda/n$ of an event. The number of steps waited, divided by $n$ to convert it to time, becomes an exponential waiting time as the steps get finer. The geometric is the discrete-time version of the exponential: both are memoryless, and rounding an exponential time down to whole units gives a geometric count.",
 'hypergeometric--normal': r"When the population, the number of successes in it and the sample are all large (and the sample is not almost the whole population), the hypergeometric count is approximately normal. Its mean is $nK/N$ and its variance is the binomial variance multiplied by the finite-population correction $(N - n)/(N - 1)$. This is the central limit theorem for sampling without replacement, used in survey sampling and for large-sample approximations to Fisher's exact test.",
 'poisson--normal': r"When the expected count $\lambda$ is large, the Poisson distribution looks like a bell curve with mean $\lambda$ and standard deviation $\sqrt\lambda$. This holds because a $\mathrm{Poisson}(\lambda)$ count is a sum of many independent Poisson counts with small means, so the central limit theorem applies.",
 'exponential--weibull': r"Raising a standard exponential variable to the power $1/k$ and multiplying by $\lambda$ gives a Weibull variable. The exponential has a constant hazard (failure rate); the power transformation makes the hazard decrease ($k < 1$) or increase ($k > 1$) over time. Note that $\lambda$ is a scale for the Weibull but a rate for the exponential.",
 'exponential--gamma': r"The total waiting time for $n$ events in a Poisson process with rate $\lambda$ is the sum of $n$ independent exponential waiting times. It has a gamma distribution with shape $n$ and rate $\lambda$ (called the Erlang distribution). The gamma family extends this to shapes that are not whole numbers, and shape 1 gives back the exponential.",
 'normal--log-normal': r"Exponentiating a normal variable gives a log-normal variable, and taking the log of a log-normal variable gives back a normal one. Because sums of many small independent effects tend to be normal, products of many small positive multiplicative effects tend to be log-normal. Response times, incomes and concentrations are often modeled this way.",
 'normal--student-t': r"Dividing a standard normal variable by an independent estimate of its standard deviation (the square root of a chi-squared variable divided by its degrees of freedom) gives a $t$ variable. This is exactly what happens in a t-test: the sample mean is standardized with the sample standard deviation instead of the true $\sigma$, which adds uncertainty and makes the tails heavier. In the other direction, as the degrees of freedom $\nu$ grow, the $t$ distribution approaches the standard normal: with a large sample the estimated standard deviation is almost exactly the true one, so the extra uncertainty disappears. In practice $t$ and normal critical values are close once $\nu$ is above about 30.",
 'normal--chi-squared': r"Square $k$ independent standard normal variables and add them up; the result has a chi-squared distribution with $k$ degrees of freedom. Sums of squared deviations behave this way, which is why the sample variance, goodness-of-fit statistics and likelihood-ratio statistics have chi-squared distributions.",
 'chi-squared--gamma': r"The chi-squared distribution with $k$ degrees of freedom is a gamma distribution with shape $k/2$ and rate $1/2$. Any gamma variable can be rescaled into a chi-squared variable, which is how gamma probabilities were once looked up in chi-squared tables.",
 'gamma--beta': r"Take two independent gamma variables with the same rate and ask what fraction of their total comes from the first one. That fraction lies between 0 and 1 and has a beta distribution whose shapes are the two gamma shapes. For example, the fraction of the total waiting time for $\alpha + \beta$ events that is spent waiting for the first $\alpha$ events is $\mathrm{Beta}(\alpha, \beta)$.",
 'categorical--bernoulli': r"The categorical distribution describes a single trial with $K$ possible outcomes, so it generalizes the yes/no Bernoulli trial. Setting the number of categories to $K = 2$ gives exactly a Bernoulli trial, with the second category playing the role of “success” (coded 1).",
 'categorical--multinomial': r"Repeat a $K$-outcome trial $n$ times and count how often each outcome occurs; the vector of counts is multinomial. This is the same step that takes the Bernoulli to the binomial, extended to more than two categories.",
 'binomial--multinomial': r"A binomial count is a multinomial with two categories (successes and failures). Going the other way, if you focus on one category of a multinomial and lump all the others together, the count in that category is binomial.",
 'poisson--multinomial': r"If several independent Poisson counts are observed and we condition on their total, the way the total is split among them is multinomial, with probabilities proportional to the rates. This is why Poisson log-linear models and multinomial models of contingency tables give the same inferences, and it underlies the conditional test that compares two Poisson rates.",
 'multinomial--chi-squared': r"Pearson's chi-squared test compares the observed count $O$ in each category with the count $E$ expected under a hypothesis. When the counts come from a multinomial and the sample is large, adding up $(O - E)^2/E$ over the $K$ categories gives a value that is approximately chi-squared with $K - 1$ degrees of freedom. One degree of freedom is lost because the counts must add up to $n$. When testing whether two categorical variables in a table are independent, the degrees of freedom are $(\text{rows} - 1) \times (\text{columns} - 1)$. A common rule of thumb is that every expected count should be at least about 5 for the approximation to be good.",
 'beta--binomial': r"In Bayesian analysis, a Beta prior on a success probability combined with binomial data gives a Beta posterior. Updating just adds the observed successes to $\alpha$ and the observed failures to $\beta$, so $\alpha$ and $\beta$ act like prior counts of successes and failures.",
 'binomial--beta-binomial': r"If the success probability is not fixed but varies from unit to unit according to a Beta distribution, the resulting counts follow a beta-binomial distribution. It has the same mean as a binomial with $p = \alpha/(\alpha + \beta)$ but a larger variance (overdispersion). It is also the Bayesian prior predictive distribution of the count; as the Beta becomes very concentrated it reduces to the binomial.",
 'beta-binomial--discrete-uniform': r"If the success probability is drawn from a flat $\mathrm{Uniform}(0, 1)$ prior, which is $\mathrm{Beta}(1, 1)$, every number of successes from 0 to $n$ is equally likely beforehand. This is the result behind Bayes' original billiard-table argument.",
 'gamma--poisson': r"A gamma prior on a Poisson rate gives a gamma posterior after observing counts. The shape increases by the total count and the rate increases by the number of observation periods, so $\alpha$ behaves like a prior total count and $\beta$ like a prior number of periods.",
 'poisson--negative-binomial': r"If each unit has its own Poisson rate and those rates vary according to a gamma distribution, the pooled counts are negative binomial. This gamma–Poisson mixture explains why negative binomial regression is used for overdispersed counts (variance larger than the mean), such as spike counts or RNA-seq reads. When the rates stop varying (very large $r$), it becomes Poisson again.",
 'dirichlet--multinomial': r"The Dirichlet is to the multinomial what the Beta is to the binomial. A Dirichlet prior on the category probabilities, combined with observed category counts, gives a Dirichlet posterior whose parameters are the prior pseudo-counts plus the observed counts.",
 'dirichlet--beta': r"A Dirichlet with two categories is a Beta distribution. With more categories, the probability of any single category (lumping all the others together) still follows a Beta distribution.",
 'gamma--dirichlet': r"Draw $K$ independent gamma variables with the same rate and divide each by their total; the resulting proportions follow a Dirichlet distribution. This is the standard way to simulate Dirichlet draws, and it generalizes the gamma-to-beta relationship to more than two parts.",
 'gamma--inverse-gamma': r"The reciprocal of a gamma variable has an inverse-gamma distribution with the same shape; the gamma rate becomes the inverse-gamma scale. In Bayesian models, a gamma prior on a precision ($1/\text{variance}$) is the same as an inverse-gamma prior on the variance.",
 'inverse-gamma--normal': r"An inverse-gamma prior on the variance of normal data with a known mean gives an inverse-gamma posterior. The shape grows by half the sample size and the scale grows by half the sum of squared deviations. (For the mean of normal data with a known variance, a normal prior is conjugate; when both are unknown, the normal–inverse-gamma prior is used.)",
 'inverse-gamma--student-t': r"A Student's $t$ variable is a normal variable whose variance is itself random, drawn from an inverse-gamma distribution. Averaging normals over uncertain variances produces heavier tails. This is why the posterior predictive distribution under a normal model with unknown variance is a $t$ distribution, and how robust $t$ likelihoods are fitted in Bayesian software. (On the map this arrow starts at the mixing distribution; the other mixture arrows, Binomial to Beta-binomial and Poisson to Negative binomial, start at the base distribution and name the mixing distribution in their label.)",
 'student-t--cauchy': r"With one degree of freedom ($\nu = 1$) the $t$ distribution is the standard Cauchy distribution. Its tails are so heavy that it has no mean or variance.",
 'normal--cauchy': r"Dividing one independent standard normal variable by another gives a standard Cauchy variable. Because the denominator is often close to zero, the ratio occasionally takes huge values, which produces the heavy tails. Both normals must be centered at zero; with non-zero means the ratio is not Cauchy.",
 'chi-squared--f': r"The ratio of two independent chi-squared variables, each divided by its degrees of freedom, has an F distribution. In ANOVA and regression, the F statistic compares the variance explained by an effect with the residual variance in exactly this form.",
 'student-t--f': r"Squaring a $t$ statistic gives an F statistic with 1 numerator degree of freedom. This is why a two-group ANOVA gives the same p-value as a two-sided two-sample t-test.",
 'f--beta': r"An F statistic can be converted to a number between 0 and 1 that has a Beta distribution. In regression this quantity is the proportion of variance explained ($R^2$, or partial $\eta^2$) under the null hypothesis, so the null distribution of $R^2$ is Beta.",
 'binomial--normal': r"With many trials and a success probability that is not close to 0 or 1, the binomial count is approximately normal with mean $np$ and variance $np(1-p)$. This is the oldest form of the central limit theorem and justifies the z-test for a proportion.",
 'uniform--exponential': r"Taking minus the log of a uniform random number and dividing by $\lambda$ produces an exponential random number. This is inverse-CDF sampling: the exponential CDF $F(x) = 1 - e^{-\lambda x}$ has inverse $F^{-1}(u) = -\ln(1 - u)/\lambda$, and because $1 - U$ is also uniform on $(0, 1)$, $-\ln(U)/\lambda$ has the same distribution. In general, plugging a uniform variable into the inverse of a distribution's CDF produces a draw from that distribution.",
 'beta--uniform': r"The Beta distribution with both shapes equal to 1 is flat on $[0, 1]$, which is the standard uniform. It is the usual “no preference” prior for a probability. In the other direction, if you draw $n$ uniform random numbers and sort them, the $k$-th smallest follows a $\mathrm{Beta}(k,\, n - k + 1)$ distribution.",
 'uniform--discrete-uniform': r"Stretching a continuous uniform number to cover $n$ equal bins and rounding down gives a discrete uniform integer; this is how a computer rolls a die. In the other direction, a discrete uniform on a very fine grid, rescaled to $[0, 1]$, looks like the continuous uniform.",
 'uniform--logistic': r"The log-odds (logit) of a uniform random number has a standard logistic distribution, because the logit is the inverse of the logistic CDF (the sigmoid). This connects the logistic distribution to the logit link used in logistic regression.",
 'gumbel--logistic': r"The difference of two independent Gumbel variables with the same scale is logistic. In random-utility models of choice, if each option's utility has independent Gumbel noise, the probability of choosing option 1 over option 2 is a logistic function of the utility difference, which gives the logit choice rule.",
 'exponential--gumbel': r"Minus the log of a standard exponential variable is a standard Gumbel variable. The Gumbel also describes the maximum of many independent exponential variables after subtracting $\ln n$, which is the prototype of extreme-value theory.",
 'exponential--laplace': r"The difference of two independent exponential variables with the same rate is Laplace (double exponential): an exponential tail on each side of zero. Conversely, the absolute distance of a Laplace variable from its center is exponential.",
 'exponential--pareto': r"Exponentiating an exponential variable (and multiplying by the minimum $x_m$) gives a Pareto variable; taking logs goes back. So the Pareto is to the exponential what the log-normal is to the normal, and its power-law tail comes from the exponential tail on the log scale.",
 'normal--ex-gaussian': r"Adding an independent exponential delay to a normal variable gives an ex-Gaussian variable. In response-time research the normal part is often read as a typical processing time and the exponential part as occasional slow responses, although the components do not map cleanly onto specific mental processes.",
 'exponential--ex-gaussian': r"Seen from the exponential side, the ex-Gaussian is an exponential waiting time blurred by normal noise and shifted by $\mu$. Its density is the convolution of the two component densities.",
 'cauchy--half-cauchy': r"Folding a Cauchy distribution centered at zero onto the positive half-line gives the half-Cauchy. It is used as a prior for standard deviations because it puts most of its mass near zero but allows occasional large values.",
 'normal--multivariate-normal': r"The multivariate normal extends the normal to several correlated variables. It can be built by taking independent standard normals and applying a linear transformation; in one dimension ($d = 1$) it is the ordinary normal. Every single variable, and every weighted sum of variables, from a multivariate normal is itself normal.",
 'multivariate-normal--chi-squared': r"The Mahalanobis distance $D_M$ measures how far a point $\mathbf x$ is from the mean $\boldsymbol\mu$ after accounting for the variances and correlations in $\Sigma$ (it is ordinary distance after the data are rescaled to be uncorrelated with unit variance). The squared Mahalanobis distance of a multivariate normal point from its mean has a chi-squared distribution with $d$ degrees of freedom. This gives confidence ellipses, multivariate outlier tests, and the large-sample chi-squared distribution of Wald tests.",
 'von-mises--normal': r"When the von Mises distribution is highly concentrated (large $\kappa$), the angles stay close to the mean direction, the circle looks locally like a straight line, and the distribution becomes approximately normal with variance $1/\kappa$.",
 'von-mises--uniform': r"With zero concentration ($\kappa = 0$) the von Mises distribution has no preferred direction: every angle on the circle is equally likely. In working-memory mixture models this uniform component represents random guessing.",
 'normal--wald': r"Accumulate evidence in many small, normally distributed steps that drift on average at rate $v$ toward a threshold $a$, with noise of standard deviation $\sigma$ per unit time ($B(t)$ is standard Brownian motion, a continuous random walk). The time at which the running total first reaches the threshold has a Wald (inverse Gaussian) distribution with mean $a/v$. This is the single-boundary version of the drift-diffusion model of response times. When the shape $\lambda$ is large relative to the mean, the Wald itself is approximately normal.",
 'logistic--bernoulli': r"Logistic regression can be written as a latent-variable model: a hidden continuous score $\eta$ plus logistic noise $\varepsilon$ is compared with zero, and the binary response is 1 when the result is positive. The probability of a 1 is then the logistic (sigmoid) function of $\eta$. Using normal noise instead gives probit regression.",
 'gumbel--categorical': r"If each option's value is perturbed by independent Gumbel noise and the option with the highest noisy value is chosen, the choice probabilities follow the softmax rule. The Gumbel scale $\beta$ plays the role of the softmax temperature. This links random-utility theory, reinforcement-learning choice models and the Gumbel-max sampling trick.",
}

